<p>If your accountant keeps the books in QuickBooks Online, HardPoint can give them a month of your figures ready to import, so nobody types
  sales in by hand. Owners, managers and accountants can download it.</p>

<h2>Before the first export: match the account names</h2>
<p>Open <strong>Reports › QuickBooks export</strong>. Under <strong>Accounts in QuickBooks</strong> is a list of each kind of money (cash,
  M-Pesa, Airtel Money, card, what customers owe, sales, VAT on sales, cost of sales, stock and so on) and the QuickBooks account it goes to.
  Ask your accountant for the names in their chart of accounts, type them in exactly, and press <strong>Save account names</strong>. Blank
  boxes keep the usual QuickBooks name shown in grey. You only need to do this once.</p>

<h2>Downloading a month</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Choose the <strong>Month</strong> and press <strong>Download for QuickBooks</strong>.</li>
  <li>You get a ZIP file. Send it to your accountant, or open it yourself.</li>
</ol>
<p>Inside the ZIP:</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>README.txt</code>: the import steps, written for the person doing them.</li>
  <li><code>customers.csv</code> and <code>suppliers.csv</code>: your customers and suppliers, to import first so every name in the
    journal already exists in QuickBooks.</li>
  <li><code>journal.csv</code> (or <code>journal-1.csv</code>, <code>journal-2.csv</code>… for a busy month): the journal entries. Each
    file stays under QuickBooks' limit of 1,000 lines, and an entry is never split between files.</li>
  <li><code>not-in-the-journal.csv</code>, only if needed: anything that couldn't go in the journal, with the reason.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Importing into QuickBooks Online</h2>
<ol>
  <li>In QuickBooks, import <code>customers.csv</code> as Customers and <code>suppliers.csv</code> as Suppliers.</li>
  <li>Then import each journal file under <strong>Settings › Import data › Journal entries</strong>. Dates are written day/month/year.</li>
  <li>Check the totals against HardPoint's <strong>Sales and margin</strong> and <strong>Tax (VAT)</strong> reports for the same month.</li>
</ol>

<h2>What's in the journal</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Sales</strong>, one entry per branch per day: the money taken by each payment method, sales before VAT, VAT on sales, and
    the cost of what was sold taken out of stock.</li>
  <li><strong>Voids and returns</strong>, reversing their sales, with goods put back into stock at their cost.</li>
  <li><strong>Deposits</strong> on orders, and <strong>customers paying</strong> their accounts.</li>
  <li><strong>Supplier invoices</strong> with VAT on purchases, and <strong>payments to suppliers</strong>. Invoices in dollars or another
    currency are converted at the rate they were entered at.</li>
</ul>
<p>HardPoint sends journal entries rather than invoices because QuickBooks won't import invoices once VAT is set up, which it is for every
  VAT-registered shop.</p>

<h2>What gets left out, and why</h2>
<p>A sale whose payments don't add up to its total, or a payment to a supplier in a foreign currency, can't be turned into a balanced
  entry. They're listed in <code>not-in-the-journal.csv</code> with the date, amount and reason, for your accountant to enter by hand.</p>
<p>Export each month once. Importing the same month twice puts the entries in QuickBooks twice.</p>
